<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">War Jabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1040</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as War Jaabi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">War Jabi was a ruler of Takrur in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> valley and one of the first West African kings to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, according to the geographer al-Bakri. He imposed Islamic law on his subjects, and his son Labi fought alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudala">Gudala</a> in 1056.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>War Jabi, in Serer Waar Jaabi, War Jaabi or War-Dyabe, belonged to the Manna dynasty, which had ruled Takrur since the early 800s. He was the son and successor of a man named Rabis. Islam had been brought to Takrur by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soninke">Soninke</a> merchants and had spread widely there before he became king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>War Jabi converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and forced his subjects to convert, introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> law in the kingdom in 1035 and making Islam the religion of the state. The change brought the state economic gains and closer political ties with the Muslim states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, which would matter in later conflicts with the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. He also waged a holy war against the King of Sila.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> of Takrur, landowners and the local agricultural people, refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in favour of their traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer-religion">Serer religion</a>. The enforcement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> drove them out of the country, and they migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>War Jabi died in 433 Hijri, 1040 or 1041 in the Gregorian calendar, and was succeeded by his son Lebi ibn War Jabi, who went on to play a key part in the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> and their rule in Al Andalus. War Jabi's embrace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> may have given impetus and inspiration to the Almoravid movement that arose among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> in the decades after his death.</p><p>Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> he is remembered with hostility. His name, War, means to kill, or death, in the Serer language. Ultra-conservative Serer, above all in the former Serer Kingdom of Sine, use the expressions the spurns of War and the spurns of Leb, after War Jabi and his son, for those of Arab descent and for other Senegambian groups they see as having helped to spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War Jabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1030 to 1035, under the Muslim leader <b>War Jabi</b>, the Serers refused forced conversion and Sharia law; they were defeated, and the first Serer exodus from Tekrur followed, south to where they mixed with the Diola.</blockquote><a href="/religion/serer-religion">Serer religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruler <b>War Jabi</b>, who died around 1040, accepted Islam and imposed Islamic law, and his son Labi fought alongside the Almoravid Empire against rebels in the western Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-takrur">Kingdom of Takrur</a></li></ul></section>
